<p>Cuba and the United States are investigating a deadly confrontation at sea after Cuban border forces exchanged gunfire with a Florida-registered speedboat in Cuban waters on Wednesday. Four people on board were killed and six were wounded. U.S. officials said at least one of those killed was an American citizen, and that at least one U.S. citizen was among the wounded and detained by Cuban authorities. Cuba’s Interior Ministry said the boat carried armed passengers it described as U.S.-based Cuban nationals. It alleged they fired first and were trying to infiltrate the island “for terrorist purposes,” and said authorities seized weapons and gear. U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio said the U.S. was investigating and working to confirm the nationalities of those involved, and stressed it was not a U.S. government operation. In a potentially constructive note amid the sharp rhetoric, Cuba said the U.S. had shown “willingness” to cooperate with the probe and that communication channels remained open.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Nv8MP6aPP-saNeoPLJRVNUVnCrcv7op9RS-kvXqwLn1rchAJh-j_LHZYrzYypGlxTQfNNzwdJuOd383YtrdeBeW6Mwj8HDZOvNv9AdDWKFmB-tFFFNc4kEeoDp87UuSUSo9-LflwnfK50E6k_ELXqOPddyAISu_QCjh-lHlsi76oY5PcMbtQ7Q' alt='Cuban coast guard ships docked at the Port of Havana on Thursday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Named victim: CBS News identified one of those killed as Michael Ortega Casanova, and said his brother described him as driven by a quest to free Cuba.</li><li>Cuba’s criticism: Cuba criticized the U.S. for allowing anti-Cuban groups to operate with “impunity,” as wounded survivors recovered under guard at a provincial hospital ahead of detention proceedings.</li><li>Alleged equipment: Cuba said it seized firearms, Molotov cocktails, bulletproof vests, and camouflage gear from the speedboat, casting the incident as an armed incursion.</li><li>Injuries reported: Cuba said gunfire from the boat wounded a border-guard commander, prompting the exchange of fire.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cuban Interior Ministry: Cuba said passengers on the Florida-registered boat fired on border guards and tried to infiltrate the island “for terrorist purposes,” and that survivors were detained after receiving medical care. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/cuba-says-4-killed-in-speedboat-shooting-were-were-attempting-to-infiltrate-the-country'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>U.S. State Department: Secretary of State Marco Rubio said the U.S. was investigating the incident and that it was not a U.S. government operation or staffed by U.S. personnel. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/cuba-us-florida-boat-shooting'>The Week</a>)</li><li>Cuban government (Díaz-Canel): President Miguel Díaz-Canel wrote that Cuba would “defend itself with determination and firmness” after the incident. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/26/what-to-know-cuba-boat-attack'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>New York Times reporting: The New York Times said Cuba’s version of events has been questioned after a man identified as being on the boat later appeared in Miami, raising doubts about the passenger list and timeline. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/26/world/americas/cuba-gunfight-boat-miami.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>WSWS analysis: World Socialist Web Site framed the incident as Cuba repelling an armed provocation, and linked it to what it described as Washington’s broader pressure campaign against the island. (<a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/02/27/tlck-f27.html'>World Socialist Web Site</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/american-among-those-killed-speedboat-cuban-waters-white-house-confirms/'>American among those killed on speedboat in Cuban waters, White House confirms</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/american-citizen-among-those-killed-by-cuban-coast-guard-in-shootout-13984176.html'>American citizen 